[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Flows, StateFlow, SharedFlow, and Cold vs Hot Streams in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous programming in Android has evolved significantly with Kotlin Coroutines. At the heart of reactive data streams in modern Android development are Flows. This tutorial explores Kotlin Flows, the specialized StateFlow and SharedFlow, and the fundamental differences between cold and hot streams."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Cold Streams vs. Hot Streams"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into specific implementations, it is crucial to understand the architectural difference between cold and hot data streams."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cold Streams (Standard Flow): Data is produced inside the flow, and each collector triggers the execution of the flow builder from the beginning. They are passive and do not emit values until someone collects them."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hot Streams (StateFlow, SharedFlow): Data is produced independently of whether there are any active collectors. They remain active in memory, broadcasting values to any current or future subscribers."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Standard Flow (Cold Stream)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A standard Flow is a cold asynchronous data stream that sequentially emits values and completes normally or with an exception. Let us look at a basic implementation."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"fun simpleFlow(): Flow<Int> = flow {
    for (i in 1..3) {
        delay(1000)
        emit(i)
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To consume a cold flow, a collector must be attached using terminal operators like collect or collectLatest within a coroutine scope."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"lifecycleScope.launch {
    simpleFlow().collect { value ->
        Log.d("Flow", "Received: $value")
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"StateFlow (Hot Stream for State)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"StateFlow is a hot flow that emits current and new updates to its collectors. It requires an initial state value and always retains the most recent value in memory. This makes it the ideal replacement for LiveData in modern MVVM architectures."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class MainViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow<UiState>(UiState.Loading)
    val uiState: StateFlow<UiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()

    fun loadData() {
        _uiState.value = UiState.Success("Data loaded successfully")
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"When collecting StateFlow in an Android UI lifecycle, use repeatOnLifecycle to prevent memory leaks and unnecessary background processing."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"lifecycleScope.launch {
    repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) {
        viewModel.uiState.collect { state ->
            render(state)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"SharedFlow (Hot Stream for Events)"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"SharedFlow is a hot stream that can emit values to multiple subscribers. Unlike StateFlow, it does not require an initial value and is highly configurable regarding replay counts and buffer overflow strategies. It is commonly used for UI one-time events like navigation or showing snackbars."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"class EventViewModel : ViewModel() {
    private val _eventFlow = MutableSharedFlow<String>(replay = 0, extraBufferCapacity = 1)
    val eventFlow: SharedFlow<String> = _eventFlow.asSharedFlow()

    fun triggerEvent(message: String) {
        _eventFlow.tryEmit(message)
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Best Practices"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use standard Flow for one-off operations, database queries via Room, or network requests transformed into streams."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use StateFlow for holding UI state that needs to survive configuration changes and maintain a single source of truth."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use SharedFlow for UI events, actions, or broadcasting messages to multiple listeners without maintaining state persistence."}]
